 CPU/REGISTER ORGANIZATION
CPU/Register Organization means how the registers inside the CPU are arranged and worked together to process data quickly.
Simple explanation:
The CPU takes the needed data from memory and keeps it in the registers.
so in registers it stores the data temporarily.
The ALU does the calculations (like add or sub) using the data in the registers.
Then answer is kept in a register and can be saved back to the memory .
Then Control Unit (CU) tells all the parts what to do and when to do it.
Example of CPU/Register Organization:
Suppose we want to add 5 + 3.
First thing the Register R1 stores 5.
then Register R2 stores 3.
The ALU adds the two numbers (5 + 3 = 8).
The result 8 is stored in the Register R3 or saved to the memory.
The Control Unit (CU) controls all these steps which are done.
